00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:07.100
In this video, we'll figure out how to get gradient descent to work with regularized linear regression.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.100
Let's jump in.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:14.580
Here's a cost function we've come up with in the last video for regularized linear regression.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.760
The first part is the usual squared error cost function.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:25.580
And now you have this additional regularization term, where lambda is the regularization parameter.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:32.500
And you'd like to find parameters w and b that minimize the regularized cost function.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.620
Previously, we were using gradient descent for the original cost function, just the first

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.340
term before we added that second regularization term.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:49.300
And previously, we had the following gradient descent algorithm, which is that we repeatedly

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:56.260
update the parameters wj and b for j equals 1 through n according to this formula, and

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.700
b is also updated similarly.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.180
Again, alpha is a very small positive number called the learning rate.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:09.420
In fact, the updates for regularized linear regression look exactly the same, except that

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.660
now the cost j is defined a bit differently.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:21.340
Previously, the derivative of j with respect to wj was given by this expression over here,

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:27.300
and the derivative with respect to b was given by this expression over here.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.900
Now that we've added this additional regularization term, the only thing that changes is that

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:39.140
the expression for the derivative with respect to wj ends up with one additional term.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:44.260
This plus lambda over m times wj.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:49.320
And in particular, for the new definition of the cost function j, these two expressions

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:56.540
over here, these are the new derivatives of j with respect to wj, and the derivative

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.060
of j with respect to b.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:04.460
Recall that we don't regularize b, so we're not trying to shrink b.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:10.580
That's why the updated b remains the same as before, whereas the updated w changes because

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:16.740
the regularization term causes us to try to shrink wj.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:21.780
So let's take these definitions for the derivatives and put them back into the expression

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:29.060
on the left to write out the gradient descent algorithm for regularized linear regression.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:35.060
So to implement gradient descent for regularized linear regression, this is what you would

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.860
have your code do.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:42.820
Here's the update for wj for j equals 1 through n, and here's the update for b.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:50.460
And as usual, please remember to carry out simultaneous updates for all of these parameters.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:55.780
Now in order for you to get this algorithm to work, this is all you need to know.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.560
But what I'd like to do in the remainder of this video is to go over some optional

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:05.740
material to convey a slightly deeper intuition about what this formula is actually doing,

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:10.100
as well as chat briefly about how these derivatives are derived.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.500
The rest of this video is completely optional.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.780
It's completely okay if you skip the rest of this video.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.780
But if you have a strong interest in math, then stick with me.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.180
It's always nice to hang out with you here.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.260
And through these equations, perhaps you can build a deeper intuition about what the math

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.700
and what the derivatives are doing as well.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.380
So let's take a look.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:37.620
Let's look at the update rule for wj and rewrite it in another way.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:49.380
We're updating wj as 1 times wj minus alpha times lambda over m times wj.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:56.660
So I've moved the term from the end to the front here, and then minus alpha times 1 over

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.380
m, and then the rest of that term over there.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.620
So we just rearranged the terms a little bit.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:13.500
And if we simplify, then we're saying that wj is updated as wj times 1 minus alpha times

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:20.020
lambda over m minus alpha times this other term over here.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:26.380
And you might recognize this second term as the usual gradient descent update for unregularized

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.140
linear regression.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:32.700
This is the update for linear regression before we had regularization.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.420
And this is the term we saw in week two of this course.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:43.260
And so the only change when you add regularization is that instead of wj being set to be equal

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:53.420
to wj minus alpha times this term is now w times this number minus the usual update.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.520
So this is what we had in week one of this course.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.900
So what is this first term over here?

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:05.660
Well, alpha is a very small positive number, say 0.01.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:11.340
Lambda is usually a small number, say one or maybe 10.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:18.220
Let's say lambda is one for this example, and m is the training set size, say 50.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:28.820
And so when you multiply alpha lambda over m, say 0.01 times 1 divided by 50, this term

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:34.940
ends up being a small positive number, say 0.0002.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:40.820
And thus, 1 minus alpha lambda over m is going to be a number just slightly less than 1.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:44.020
In this case, 0.9998.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:49.500
And so the effect of this term is that on every single iteration of gradient descent,

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.780
you're taking wj and multiplying it by 0.9998.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:06:00.220
That is by some number slightly less than 1 before carrying out the usual update.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:06.140
So what regularization is doing on every single iteration is you're multiplying w by a number

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:13.380
slightly less than 1, and that has the effect of shrinking the value of wj just a little bit.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:18.980
So this gives us another view on why regularization has the effect of shrinking the parameters

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:22.220
wj a little bit on every iteration.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:25.740
And so that's how regularization works.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:30.700
If you're curious about how these derivative terms were computed, I have just one last optional

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:36.060
slide that goes through just a little bit of the calculation of the derivative term.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:40.140
Again, this slide and the rest of this video are completely optional,

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.660
meaning you won't need any of this to do the practice labs and the quizzes.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:48.220
So let's step through quickly the derivative calculation.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:54.540
The derivative of j with respect to wj looks like this.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:15.570
Recall that f of x for linear regression is defined as w dot x plus b, or w dot product x plus b.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:21.170
And it turns out that by the rules of calculus, the derivatives look like this.

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:36.210
This is 1 over 2m times the sum i equals 1 through m of w dot x plus b minus y times 2xj

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:44.770
plus the derivative of the regularization term, which is lambda over 2m times 2wj.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:52.050
Notice that the second term does not have the summation term from j equals 1 through n anymore.

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:57.570
The 2s cancel out here and here, and also here and here.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:02.370
And so it simplifies to this expression over here.

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:11.700
And finally, remember that wx plus b is f of x.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:15.780
And so you can rewrite it as this expression down here.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:22.820
So this is why this expression is used to compute the gradient in regularized linear regression.

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:28.100
So you now know how to implement regularized linear regression.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.900
Using this, you really reduce overfitting when you have a lot of features and a relatively small

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:38.900
training set. And this should let you get linear regression to work much better on many problems.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:45.140
In the next video, we'll take this regularization idea and apply it to logistic regression.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:51.380
To avoid overfitting for logistic regression as well. Let's take a look at that in the next video.
